1 OCCU alias: Knut Nilsson Finne
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Knut brukte heile Finnegodtset. Han døydde i 1739. Han skulde bera ei høibyrd over elvi, datt so uti og bleiv
2 CONT tatt av elvi. Saman med fleire var han vald på tinget i 1713 til å likna ut dagskatten, men ingen av dei vilde gjera det, for det var stort misnøgje med denne skatteskrivingi. I 1713 er tinglese ein obligasjon frå Knut til David M
2 CONC arkusson på 200 riksdalar, der Knut har gjeve pant i 2 laup i Seim, 2 pund 7 mark i Mølster og 18 mark i Finne. I 1714 åtte Knut 2 laup 2 pund 10  mark i Finne, Knut Raustad 1 pund 10 mark og enkja etter Klas Miltzow 1 pund 9  mar
2 CONC k i Finne. Bruttomidelen var då 214 riksdalar og ved skiftet etter Margreta i 1763 106 riksdalar. 23 JAN 2014 20:52:32  15 JAN 2012 19:50:33 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Referansenummer: MH:I522
2 CONT 23 JAN 2014 20:52:32  15 JAN 2012 01:48:02 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT In a document dated 1706, one finds that Knut Nilsson Finne was the verger for the churches in Voss parish.  In the same document it states that there was a big upheaval regarding this.  A lot of thewoodwork had to be repaired.  H
2 CONC e obtained the timber from a 'a side valley in Raundalen three and a half miles away'.  Then he built a sawmill and did the required work.  It was also Knut who installed the pews in the church about 1720. Knut was a discriminatin
2 CONC g and skillful artisan and a valuable man in the district.  His wife, Margreta Miltzow, "Bailiff's daughter", was an authoritative female,one who knew what she was worth and where she was from.  Thus it was told that after the 'fl
2 CONC ap' about the church pews, and each farm had been assigned its pew (some farms shared the same pew) and theFinne pew was in the tenth row, Margreta didn't think twice about it. The first time she went to church after the repairs w
2 CONC ere completed she went directly to the fourth pew and sat down.  "My husbandinstalled these pews andI'll sit wherever I want to", she is quoted to have said. Thus it came to pass that the Finne pew was the fourth in line. Margreta
2 CONC  was not timid.  She represented her husbandin court against several claimants who had a couple of major cases against him.Inthose times it is told, there was once such gale weather that there was ice way out in the fjords so that
2 CONC  one coulddrive all the way to Bergen. During this storm Margreta took a trip to town and wore out two horses in the process.  Thus say the legends.A little poem about Margreta that has survived by word ofmouth until this day: I c
2 CONC ame to the wedding farm,     I met the bridegroom's father;     He invited me in.     When I was inside     There sat Knut Finne Drinking with his guests.     She, Margreta Finne,     took off in a hast to get cake and lefse.     
2 CONC She told me to eat;     Then she wanted to know     All the gossip around. Legend has it that it was a traveling hobo that coined these lines. In such a way he wanted to describe what had happened to him on a trip through Finne at
2 CONC  wedding time.
2 CONT 23 JAN 2014 20:52:32  15 JAN 2012 19:50:33 
