1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Bio from FindAGrave==
2 CONT Civil War soldier Knut Iverson (Vike) of Company F, 15th Wisconsin Infantry. Born in Voss, Norway, he was a resident of the Town of Dane, Dane County, Wisconsin when he enlisted for three years on 18 Jan 1862 at Dane County. He wa
2 CONC s Mustered-In at Madison, Wisconsin on 27 Jan 1862. He was a private, was age 39, and married at the time of his enlistment. He was assigned as a guard with the commissary supply train for a while in 1862. He died 31 Dec 1862 at S
2 CONC tone's River, Tennessee.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/62731838/knut-iverson
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Bio from Holtan Family History document==
2 CONT Knud Iversen (son of Iver Vike), age 23 married Dorthea Rokne, age 21 in 1846 in Voss, Norway. After their first five children were born (Ivar in 1847, Ellan in 1848, Anna in 1851, Knute in 1853, Nels in 1856) they emigrated to Am
2 CONC erica in 1856, settling first in Columbia County, Wisconsin.  Three more children were born (Severt in 1858. Erick in 1860, Bertha in 1862). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Knud enlisted into Company F, 15th Wisconsin Infantry from Dane County on Jan 18th, 1862 to fight in the Civil War. Their company was sworn in at Camp Randall near Madison on Feb 14th. They left Wisconsin on Mar 2nd and arrived at
2 CONC  Bird’s Point, MO (across Mississippi River from Cairo, IL) on Mar 14th. They joined Union forces to fight the Seige of Island #10 ending in Union victory on Apr 8th. From there, moving to the southeast along the railroad from St.
2 CONC  Louis to Atlanta they encountered several battles including Perryville, KY and Nashville, TN. They arrived at the Stones River near Murfreesboro, TN on the evening of December 30th, 1862. The temperature was slightly below freezi
2 CONC ng and it was raining. The Confederate Army was camped very nearby. The bloody battle began at sunrise of December 31st, 1862. Knud was killed that day and is buried at the Stones River National Battleground Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle between the Confederate Army of 35,000 led by General Braxton Bragg and the Union Army of 41,400 led by General William Rosecrans ended Jan 2, 1863 with a narrow but major Union victory.  Casualties were Union Army 12,9
2 CONC 06 and Confederate Army 11,739.
2 CONT
2 CONT Back home, Knud’s widow, Dorthea remarried in 1865 to Knud’s good friend Anders Lee and moved near Eau Claire, WI. Dorthea and all eight children (ages 3 to 18) changed their last names from Iversen to Lee. The oldest child, Iver 
2 CONC changed his name to Edwin Newton. The youngest child, Bertha married Charles Thompson and remained in the Eau Claire area. Ellen, Anna, Knute and Erick migrated to Minnesota, Nels to Nebraska and Severt to California. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Info provided by Don Holtan)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/62731838/knut-iverson<br/>
2 CONT <br/>Civil War soldier Knut Iverson (Vike) of Company F, 15th Wisconsin Infantry. Born in Voss, Norway, he was a resident of the Town of Dane, Dane County, Wisconsin when he enlisted for three years on 18 Jan 1862 at Dane County. 
2 CONC He was Mustered-In at Madison, Wisconsin on 27 Jan 1862. He was a private, was age 39, and married at the time of his enlistment. He was assigned as a guard with the commissary supply train for a while in 1862. He died 31 Dec 1862
2 CONC  at Stone's River, Tennessee.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: Wisconsin Civil War history
2 CONT ---
2 CONT See also "Voss Norway" [link]: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voss
2 CONT ---
2 CONT "The Battle of Stones River began on the last day of 1862 and was one of the bloodiest conflicts of the Civil War. The battle produced important military and political gains for the Union."
2 CONT
2 CONT See also "Stones River National Battlefield (U.S. National Park Service)" [link]: www.nps.gov/stri/
