1 NAME Johannes Brynjulvsen /Berge/
2 GIVN Johannes Brynjulvsen
2 SURN Berge
2 _MARNM Berge
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 7 FEB 1832
2 ADDR
3 CITY Berge, Bulken, Voss
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 JAN 1916
2 PLAC Deerfield
2 ADDR
3 CITY Deerfield
3 STAE Wisconsin
3 CTRY United States of America
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Translated from Historie om udvandringen fra Voss pg. 439-440:
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes Brynjulvson Berge, born on Berge, Voss, the 7th January 1832, son of Brynjulv Johannesson Hovda and Mari Gulleiksdatter Saeve, farm worker on Berge. Mari's ancestors include  sherriff Mads Torbjornson Saeve of Dal and fem
2 CONC ale ancestor Inger Clausdatter Miltzow. Johannes bought 160 acres of land from his brother Brynjulv, who one time bought it from the government. He has lived on this land since. In 1855 he married Angelina Mikkelsdatter Erdahl (Ha
2 CONC rdanger). He was very involved in town affairs, in which he took interest. For five terms he served as town treasurer, for four terms as assessor, and was many times a member of town committees as supervisor. He was a republican, 
2 CONC and he belonged to the Norwegian Lutheran church. In 1890 he built himself a house on his land in Deerfield and lived there until his death in about 1916. He had five children: Brynjulv and Michael, farmers in Deerfield, whiere Mi
2 CONC chael died in 1921, leaving a wife and seven children; Nicolay in Vernon county, Wis.; Thorbjorg, first married to Nils O. Gilderhus and after his death to Peter B. Grinde, Deerfield. She is dead. Eli was married to Andrew Holmen 
2 CONC and traveled to Saint Croix county, Wisconsin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Brynjulv Brynjulvson Berge, Johannes's brother, came to America before 1850, but we don't know when. He died on the Koshkonong prairie in August 1858.
2 CONT
2 CONT Britha Brynjulvsdatter Berge, the aforementioned's sister, came to Koshkonong in 1850. She was married there to Nicolay M. Erdahl, a brother of Johannes Berge's wife. One son, Bertinus Erdahl, farmer in Deerfield, is known. Britha
2 CONC  is dead. 
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Dane County: biographical and genealogical, Volume 1 By Western Historical Association p. 84-85:
2 CONT
2 CONT John B. Berge, a retired farmer, living in the village of Deerfield, was born at Voss, Norway, January 7, 1832, both his parents, Bungel and Mary (Golickson) Johnson, being natives of that country. Mr. Berge received a moderate ed
2 CONC ucation in the schools of NOrway, and in 1850 came alone to America, making his way directly to Dane county. He bought one hundred and sixty acres of land from his brother, who had purchased it from the government a short time bef
2 CONC ore, and began farming. In 1890 he bought some lots in the village of Deerfield and built the house in which he now lives. However, he still owns the farm, which is now conducted by his son. In politics he belongs to the Republica
2 CONC n party; has served five terms as town treasurer; four terms as assessor; and has several times been elected as a member of the board of supervisors. He is a member of the Norwegian Lutheran church, and is a man of high moral prin
2 CONC ciples, noted for his generosity. In the fall of 1855 he married Angeline mIchelson Erdall, daughter of Michael Erdall, and they have the following children: Bungel and Michael on the home farm in Dane county; Nickoli in Vernon co
2 CONC unty; Tena, married  Peter B. Winde of Deerfield, Ellen, married Andrew Hollman, St. Croix county. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Norwegian Pioneer Association
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes Bryngelson Berge was born the 7th of February A. D. 1832 in the parish of Voss in Bergens Stift in Norway. Emigrated to America in company with his half sister Britha in the spring of 1850, got on board of the sail ship t
2 CONC hat they came across the ATlantic Ocean on, in the city of Bergen in Norway, and after having been 7 weeks on the sea they landed in the city of New York in America. From New York to Albany they came up the Hudson river on a steam
2 CONC boat and from Albany to Buffalo they took passage on a canal boat on the Erie canal, from Buffalo to Milwaukee they came up the Lakes on a steamboat.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the fall of 1850 he went to Lafayette County Wis. in company with his half Brother Gullick who had come to America in 1847, and he worked there between 2 and 3 years in the lead mines, then he came back to Deerfield. In 1854 he
2 CONC  bought a piece of land on Section No. 33 in town of Deerfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1855 he married MIss Anguna Mikkelsdatter Erdall. She was born the 2nd March 1835 in Eidfjord in Hardanger Bergens Stift in Norway and came ot America with her parents in 1847. In 1852  his Brother Bryngel came to America and d
2 CONC ied in 1858. In 1857 parents Bryngel Johanneson Hovde and Mari Hovde, his brother Nels and his two sisters Britha and Martha came. His father died 1881, and his mother died in 1883, and his Brother Reverend Nels B. Berge died in 1
2 CONC 884 in New Hope, Portage County, Wis., he left a wife and four children to mourn his loss.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nine children was born until Mr. and Mrs. Johannes Bryngelson Berge, 3 boys and 6 girls. Bryngel, Michael, Nickolae, Mary the oldest, Mary the youngest, Forbjor, Kristi, Ellen, and Betsy Syrene. Mary the oldest died the 6th of Sep
2 CONC tember 1856, Mrs. Johannes Bryngelson Berge died the 3rd of December 1871, and left a husband and eight children to mourn her loss.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary the youngest died the 8th of June 1874, adn Kristi died the 23rd of August 1874. Mrs. JOhn Bryngelson Berge has been a farmer until lately; he resides now in the Village of Deerfield, he has been a steady and good worker, he 
2 CONC has been elected town treasurer a dnumber of times and town Assessor several years, and Supervisor of the town Board, and he was a true and faithful town Officer and attentive to his official duties, and a kind and obliging man an
2 CONC d a good Neighbor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes Bryngelson Berge died January 9th 1916.
