1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Styrk Iverson Vike came with his wife Kristi Torbjornsdtr Rene and 6 month old daughter Ellen or Eli Iverson to the Muskego Settlement in Wisconsin in 1943.  They traveled from Bergen to New York on a ship named Ju
2 CONC no.  They were traveling with the families of his brothers Sjur Ivarson Vike and Gulliek Iverson Vike.  His brother Claus (Nils) Iverson Vike had come to the settlement in 1941 to prepare for the families.Old Muskego (Historic Mar
2 CONC ker Erected 1963)
2 CONT At entrance to Norway Lutheran Church, across from Heg Park, Hwy. 36, about 5 mi. N of Waterford, Racine County
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the leadership of John Luraas, forty pioneers came to Muskego Lake from Norway in 1839, to found one of the most important settlements in Norwegian American history. After temporary setbacks, the settlement flourished here '
2 CONC  through the leadership of Even Heg, Johannes Johannsen, Soren Bache, Elling Eielsen, James Reymert, and Claus Clausen, who sent glowing reports to Norway and encouraged a large movement to this country. This settlement gave rise 
2 CONC to the first Norwegian Lutheran congregation organized in America (1843) and pub­lished the first Norwegian American newspaper. Old Muskego became well known as a mother colony to other settlements, schools, and churches springing
2 CONC  up on the new frontier. Countless wagonloads of newcomers stopped here before continuing west. Nearby Heg Park commemorates Colonel Hans C. Heg, one of Wisconsin's Civil War heroes.
