1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [March2004transfer.ged]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1849: Brynjel Johannesson Lavikhaugen sells the farm Lavikhaugen to Mikkel Jakobson Fjellanger for 150 spesiedaler. Mikkel was married to Brynjel's sister, and now had both the farms, Fjellanger and Haugen. Olav Jakobson Flatekvål
2 CONC  had rented one third of Haugen since 1837, and continued to do so, thus Mikkel ran two thirds of Haugen. During the same year, 1849, Mikkel sold half of Fjellanger to his brother Olav (Jakobsen Fjellanger) for 450 spesidaler. A s
2 CONC urvey was held, and Fjellanger was divided into two farms, each with a tax value of 12 marks of butter*. Mikkel then had the first of these farms ("bruk 1") and 2/3 of Haugen, and ran both these farms for many years, supposedly un
2 CONC til 1861, when he deeded this farm to his son. He lived on Haugen after he bought it. It was during the Christmas of 1848 that these farm trades were made. Johannes A. Lavik said this of the event: "Olav Jakobson Fjellanger, b. 18
2 CONC 11, was married to Anna Johannesdatter Brekkhus, b. 1817. They bought a farm in Abbedissen (translated: The abess) on the north end of Askøy, facing the Herdla fiord, but neither Olav nor his wife felt comfortable on these heather
2 CONC y mounds of rock. In 1848 Olav returned to Fjellanger for Christmas, and on the third day a Christmas feast was held at his brother Mikkel's farm. Present there was Mikkel and his wife, Brynjel Haugen and Anders Yksendal with thei
2 CONC r wives, and Olav. There was plenty of food and ale, but Olav never drank so much as to not be able to think clearly. While they ate and drank and caroused, Mikkel sold half of Fjellanger to his brother Olav. This was attested by 
2 CONC witnesses, and Olav was careful as to have it confirmed. On the fourth day, the same people were at a feast at Ander's in Yksendalen. There was much talk about how great it was in America. It was said that "There, the tobbaco is h
2 CONC anging on the road posts, and people can take as much as they want". While they were talking about this, Anders got the idea that he would go to America. Thus he sold his farm to Jakob Almelid, his farm hand. -The next day the sam
2 CONC e people were at a feast at Brynjel's in Haugen. Then Brynjel found out that he would join Anders and go to America, and he sold his farm Haugen to Mikkel Fjellanger. After this both the Christmas carousing and the farm trading ce
2 CONC ased for that year." Neither Anders nor Brynjel ever went to America. Anders bought a farm on Flaktveit in Åsane, and Brynjel went to Askøy and bought the farm Haugane on Hop, as mentioned in the story of Lavikhaugen.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Law of July 24th 1824: 1 mark equals 249.056 gram.
