2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/80533990/ingeborg-reine
1 FAMC @F6000000007636624242@
1 RFN geni:6000000007635680774
1 SUBM @S6000000005437460185@
1 NOTE Visited by Pastor from Fertile Luth, IA, for communion on 22 Dec, 1895
2 CONT
2 CONT      The 1880 census shows living with Ingeborg and Hans the children including son Andrew, age 5.  Niece, Violet Rye Carlson remembers mother Sophie mentioning him once, but otherwise knows nothing about him. The 1885 census of F
2 CONC ertile Township, Worth County, IA, shows Ingebor Reine (Hans died in 1882) living with children Kasper(ne)(16,) Anne(14), Jane(12), and Emma(5); IA roll #1021705, page 136, lines 25-29.  The 1895 census of Fertile Township, Worth 
2 CONC County, IA, has Ingebor H. Ranie living with daughters Anna H., Jennie H., and Sophie H. She is not listed in the Fertile Township census of 1900.
2 CONT      (remembrances by Dennis Tweed, July 14, 1991)  When daughter, Anne and her husband, Gullick Tweed went to northwest South Dakota near Faith to try a homestead, Ingeborg also went and homesteaded there.  This period of time wa
2 CONC s from 1909-1914 when she was about 70 years old.  The homesteads were in the same area, but as it was a little distance between them, her grandson, Gilman, stayed with her and was a "runner" between the two households. They had d
2 CONC ugouts built into the side of a hill with grass on top.  She had sheep so she would have wool to card and then make clothing. This was her source of income. One of Gilman's jobs was to chase the sheep off her roof. Gilman remember
2 CONC ed seeing the Indians crossing the prairie in the spring, riding their horses and pulling the poles behind the horses to carry their tepees and other belongings.
2 CONT      As it was too dry to grow crops those years (it nearly always is there), Gullick and Anne took jobs in Lyman some distance from the homestead.  Because of this distance they would stay for periods of time in Lyman (just west 
2 CONC of Chamberlain) while Grandmother Ingeborg had the children.
2 CONT      Ingeborg expressed a dislike for "bouncing around" with different children; she preferred to stay with one of her daughters.  Ingeborg lived in St. Louis park with Gullick and Ann the last 5 years of her life, always knitting
2 CONC  and making clothes for the family.
2 CONT          Six relatives, including Ingeborg, are buried in Grandview Park Cemetery in Hopkins, MN.  (Son-in-law Gullick Tweed worked in or near Hopkins and Ingeborg lived with them) They are buried in the same area (none are side b
2 CONC y side) in the far southeast of the old section of the cemetery. Buried there are Ingeborg Reine, (daughter) Anne C. Tweed and her husband, Gullick Tweed, (daughter) Sophie Rye and her husband, Elmer M. Rye, and Sophie and Elmer's
2 CONC  son, Melvin A. Rye, who died of tuberculosis.  He had been attending the Bible Institute.
1 NOTE Visited by Pastor from Fertile Luth, IA, for communion on 22 Dec, 1895
2 CONT
