1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/63503077/sjur-torbjernson-hommedal
1 FAMC @F6000000007636610096@
1 RFN geni:6000000007635614733
1 SUBM @S6000000005437460185@
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/b4/1b/cb/5f/534448607ed12bbf/7e5c6c84-671c-421a-87c2-50da8da7d24c_original.jpg?hash=ecdfb2e0b41d7e450df16932182b750860401f7911330c5d0b1fa3c01357700c.1797580799
2 FORM jpg
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE (Rene, pp. 582-583)  Sjur Torbjornson Hommedahl, born on the Hommedal farm in Vossestrand on June 11th, 1823, began training at age 18 in the shoe trade and was a shoemaker in Voss.  In 1849 he married Ingeborg Sjursdatter Selje, 
2 CONC and in 1852 they went to America.  The two first years they were in Dane County, Wisconsin, then a time in Madison where he was a shoemaker;  then they moved to Winneshiek county, Iowa, and bought land. They sold it in 1856 and mo
2 CONC ved to Goodhue county, Minnesota, where they lived on a 160-acre farm in Leon township.  He lived there until 1884, when he handed the farm over to his oldest son who married, and moved to a 100-acre farm one mile away. In the fal
2 CONC l of 1897 he moved back to live with his sons.  He died there on July 16, 1897.  He was first a member of Holden Norwegian-Lutheran congregation, but when the slavery issue began, he changed to the Emmanuel congregation of the Eva
2 CONC ngelical Lutheran (Eilsons) synod.  In 1875, when the Hauge Synod split off, he changed to the Wanamingo Conference congregation.  He was survived by two sons, Torbjorn, and Sjur.
2 CONT      Torbjorn Sjurson Hommedahl, at the time of his wedding in 1884, took over the old homestead, which was at R. 3, Cannon Falls, MN.  He was the owner of much land, and a member of Vosselaget.  Sjur Sjurson Hommedahl operated a 
2 CONC hotel in Cannon Fall, MN.
